---
import BaseLayout from '../../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../../../components/navigation/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import { buildLocalePath } from '../../../../i18n/utils';

const locale = 'en-US' as const;

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "BlogPosting",
  "headline": "Salary to Hourly: How to Convert Your Pay in 2026",
  "description": "Convert an annual salary to an hourly rate by dividing by your actual working hours: $50,000 a year is about $24.04 an hour on a 40-hour week, and $60,000 is about $28.85 — plus how part-time hours and take-home pay change the figure.",
  "datePublished": "2026-11-03",
  "dateModified": "2026-11-03",
  "author": {
    "@type": "Organization",
    "name": "PayRenTax"
  },
  "publisher": {
    "@type": "Organization",
    "name": "PayRenTax",
    "logo": {
      "@type": "ImageObject",
      "url": "https://payrentax.me/logo.svg"
    }
  },
  "inLanguage": "en-US",
  "keywords": "salary to hourly, salary to hourly calculator, convert salary to hourly rate, annual salary to hourly"
};
---

<BaseLayout
  locale={locale}
  title="Salary to Hourly: How to Convert Your Pay in 2026 | PayRenTax"
  description="Convert an annual salary to an hourly rate using your real working hours, with examples from $40,000 to $100,000, and remember that the result is gross pay, not take-home pay."
  canonical="/en-us/blog/salary-to-hourly-conversion-2026/"
>
  <Fragment slot="head">
    <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(schema)} />
  </Fragment>

  <Breadcrumbs
    locale={locale}
    trail={[
      { label: 'Blog', href: buildLocalePath(locale, 'blog') },
      { label: 'Salary to Hourly: How to Convert Your Pay in 2026', href: buildLocalePath(locale, 'blog/salary-to-hourly-conversion-2026') }
    ]}
  />

  <article class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 py-10">
    <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold text-slate-900">Salary to Hourly: How to Convert Your Pay in 2026</h1>
    <p class="mt-4 text-lg text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
      Job offers rarely use the same language. One quotes an annual salary, another quotes an
      hourly rate, and comparing them in your head is harder than it looks. The conversion
      itself is simple arithmetic — the trick is using your real working hours instead of
      assuming a standard week you do not work.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900">The basic formula</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
      Divide your annual salary by the number of hours you work in a year. For a standard
      full-time schedule of 40 hours a week for 52 weeks, that is 2,080 hours a year, so the
      formula is: hourly rate = annual salary / 2,080. If your schedule is different, use
      your own figure instead: weekly hours multiplied by the number of weeks you work in
      the year.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900">Example conversions on a 40-hour week</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
      On 2,080 hours a year, the conversions work out like this:
    </p>
    <ul class="mt-3 list-disc pl-6 text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
      <li>$40,000 a year is about $19.23 an hour</li>
      <li>$50,000 a year is about $24.04 an hour</li>
      <li>$60,000 a year is about $28.85 an hour</li>
      <li>$75,000 a year is about $36.06 an hour</li>
      <li>$100,000 a year is about $48.08 an hour</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="mt-3 text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
      The same table in reverse is just as useful: an hourly rate multiplied by 2,080 gives
      the equivalent annual salary on a full-time schedule.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900">If you do not work 40 hours</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
      Part-time schedules change the result completely, because the same salary spread over
      fewer hours is a higher hourly rate — and the same hourly rate over fewer hours is a
      lower annual salary. A $50,000 salary on a 30-hour week, for example, is 1,560 hours a
      year, which works out to about $32.05 an hour, not $24.04. Always convert with the
      hours you actually work, including any regular unpaid time off, rather than the
      textbook 2,080.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900">Gross pay is not take-home pay</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
      This conversion gives you gross pay — the figure before payroll deductions. What you
      actually keep is smaller, and it depends on your location and pay details. So use the
      hourly conversion to compare offers, then run the winning figure through a paycheck
      calculator to see the take-home amount before you decide. An offer that looks better
      per hour can still lose once deductions are counted.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900">Try it with your own numbers</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
      If your schedule is not a neat 40 hours, or you want the reverse conversion, use the
      salary-to-hourly calculator and the hourly-to-salary calculator on this site instead
      of doing the division by hand. Enter your own hours and weeks, and compare the result
      with the offer in front of you before you accept it.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-6">
      <a href="/en-us/salary/salary-to-hourly/" class="font-semibold text-accent">Salary to hourly calculator</a>
      <span class="text-slate-400"> · </span>
      <a href="/en-us/salary/hourly-to-salary/" class="font-semibold text-accent">Hourly to salary calculator</a>
      <span class="text-slate-400"> · </span>
      <a href="/en-us/salary/paycheck-calculator/" class="font-semibold text-accent">Paycheck calculator</a>
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
